<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batang Hari River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Batanghari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Batang Hari is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, flowing about 800 kilometres from the Minangkabau highlands past Jambi to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. In the first millennium it carried gold and forest products to the coast and was the home of the Melayu kingdom and the temple complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muaro-jambi">Muara Jambi</a>. Later the kingdom of Dharmasraya flourished on its upper course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batang-hari-river.jpg" alt="Batang Hari River" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Samenvloeiing_van_de_Tembesirivier_en_de_Batang_Hari_in_het_district_Rawas_Sumatra_TMnr_60002829.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
